From 8d0aa103624e98157b698c67f6bcf74b9af63478 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Karel Zak <kzak@redhat.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 18:23:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 28/32] libmount: pin the mount target for non-root users
 [CVE-2026-53613, CVE-2026-53612]

The target of a non-root mount always comes from fstab, but any
component of that path may be a symlink the user controls.
prepare_target() resolved it with mnt_resolve_path(), which follows
symlinks with root privileges, so the mount could be redirected:

	fstab:  /dev/sdb1 /mnt/usb vfat user,noauto 0 0
	user:   rmdir /mnt/usb; ln -s /etc /mnt/usb
	result: mount lands on /etc

For restricted contexts the target is now opened as it comes from fstab
with ul_open_no_symlinks() (openat2(RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS)), so a symlink
anywhere in the path is refused by the kernel, and the name is read back
from /proc/self/fd/<fd>.  The name is symlink free and normalized, and
it is the very name the kernel later reports in mountinfo, which is what
utab and non-root umount need.  This also means that a symlink in the
target path of an fstab entry is no longer usable by non-root users,
root has to write the real path.

The pinned FD is then used instead of the path by everything which
touches the target afterwards, so the path is never resolved for the
second time:

 * the new mount API attaches to the FD with MOVE_MOUNT_T_EMPTY_PATH,

 * classic mount(2) and the bind/move source use the /proc/self/fd/<fd>
   magic link, which the kernel follows directly to the pinned
   directory; there is a fallback to the plain path when /proc is not
   available,

 * X-mount.owner=, X-mount.group= and X-mount.mode= use
   fchownat(fd, "", ..., AT_EMPTY_PATH) and chmod() on the FD name
   instead of the target path,

 * X-mount.idmap= detaches the cloned mount and attaches the idmapped
   one through the FD.

Once the filesystem is attached the FD has to be re-opened, so that it
refers to the root of the new mount and not to the directory covered by
it.  This is done by the new mnt_context_finalize_target(), which also
stores the mount ID to the utab entry.  do_mount() returned directly
from exec_helper(), so for an external mount.<type> helper the FD stayed
as it was pinned before anything was mounted and the MNT_STAGE_POST
hooks worked with the underlying directory -- X-mount.owner= and friends
then modified the mount point itself and the change survived umount.
Finalize the target for a successful helper too.

Backport notes:

 * The patches are taken from the upstream stable/v2.42 branch; each of
   the commits referenced below records the corresponding master commit
   in its own message.

 * The mount ID *verification* of the re-opened target (upstream
   646fb6660 and the related hunks of 472cc3fb2 and 70dae206e) is not
   backported.  This branch has no unique mount ID (STATX_MNT_ID_UNIQUE
   is v6.8+ and fs->uniq_id does not exist here), and the reusable
   32-bit STATX_MNT_ID is not a safe identity check -- a mismatch would
   umount a mount we cannot account for.  The ID *fetch* is kept,
   because this branch uses fs->id to correlate utab with mountinfo.

 * mnt_id_from_fd(), mnt_id_from_path() and mnt_fs_fetch_ids() are
   backported as the id-only variants (no uniq_id).  They are internal;
   libmount.sym has a local: section, so unlisted symbols stay hidden
   and there is no ABI change.

 * b3879c50b is not applicable, its hunks are already covered here.

 * The pin failure is reported by DBG() only; this branch has no
   mnt_context_sprintf_mesg() and the message would not be printed by
   mnt_context_get_mount_excode() on the preparation path anyway.  The
   user sees the kernel error -- "mount failed: Too many levels of
   symbolic links" for a symlinked target.

 * mnt_context_syscall_save_status(), mnt_fs_mark_attached(),
   mnt_fs_mark_moved(), mnt_context_read_mesgs() and DBG_OBJ() do not
   exist here; the hunks are rewritten to the idioms of this branch.

